/ref 17592186544834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105853 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105853 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186544839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105853 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying an Integral Expression For the Area of a Petal of a Cosine Polar Rose" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105853 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105853 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e5d5ccdb-070b-5ce1-b8af-dddb87675bdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding the Limits of Integration For a Given Polar Region" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105983 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bed445f2-ab2d-5551-bb75-6352876e2871" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106037 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1c/1c296052cfe60b35b326029afa1efb610ce32e4e2fb3ae0d63f24e437c3bbfb2.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6c70642-adfc-517d-8652-a26cc7fbca82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106037 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta $ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $3\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106148 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106148 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20905" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106148 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106148 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/01a371be4c5a555b0d3790431111468acfed1471d16c4c4c305bcfe311ce3576.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the area enclosed by the first petal of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ),$ represented by the shaded region shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106148 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db057c50-119e-5372-a829-3c2e39e59b9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106148 #edb/ref 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits.  \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =0,\\pi \\,⟹\\,\\theta =0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Notice that this interval lies in the first quadrant, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106712 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106712 #edb/ref 1061 2223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106712 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0856f04-60ee-5909-8802-83bd60dc8021" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106712 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106717 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106717 #edb/ref 1061 2226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc3630b3-b6c0-5729-9339-955c17b0c686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106718 #edb/ref 1061 2228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106718 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2c17ef49-a79f-589f-ba64-7c3c729101b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106718 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106819 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106718 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106819 #edb/ref 1061 35741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106819 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ace7e49-8905-52e0-be0e-4dd86bba8c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106819 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1061 35801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7c91fbda-be5c-560d-bc46-59441d609e42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1064 "The area bounded by a polar curve $r = r(\\theta)$ and the rays $\\theta = \\theta_1$ and $\\theta = \\theta_2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\, .\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9059613162af33791e7edb7413dfbac9739cf24eeabd01b0033acba2b772a.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0,$ and since $r=\\sin(3\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\, .\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3,$ and the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1065 1130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "774c3094-f23a-5a9b-90f7-90e89c6b5883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107022 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1064 "When working with polar coordinates, we primarily use the following domain for the polar angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, it's sometimes a good idea to use the domain \n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\lt \\pi.\n$$\n\nChanging the domain for $\\theta$ turns out to be especially useful when a polar petal spans the first and fourth quadrants. \n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1065 5842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "529233d0-8f59-53b6-8be1-042c5b5ac84b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107126 #edb/ref 1067 "Using an Alternative Range for the Polar Angle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544097 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544097 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544097 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04aaf6f0-1ada-5467-bb53-d81bc7503a1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544103 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /12}^{\\pi /12}{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544103 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544103 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "07480ff0-ae02-581d-86f9-7d724c53aaa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544112 #edb/re